There is also a uniform moment instrument. Glue the original (z,pi)
chart to (h,x), x=1/pi, by

    z=(h-b4)*x^3-b1-b2*x-b3*x^2.

The resulting O(-3) affine-line torsor over P^1 has one-dimensional
second algebraic de Rham cohomology. For each a,b >= 0, the global form
Q^a*P^b*dQ wedge dP is exact. A coefficient formula for its period is

    Res_{x=0} [ integral_0^L Q^a P^b J dL ]
                 at L=b1*x^-3+b2*x^-2+b3*x^-1 = 0.

For a fixed (a,b) this involves only L-jets through 2a+3b+4. The first
moment is tau*b3+c*b1*b2=0, already the boundary identity. The tested
P-moment on b3=b1=0 is also implied by the spine. The exact necessary
Q,P,Q^2 moments and their derivation are in moment-residues-notes.md
and moment_residues.log. A Bezout identity for U',V' in K[h] cannot be
used with coefficients in K[U,V] without handling the boundary
collisions above. The moment approach is a possible additional tool,\+not an established vanishing argument.
